  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe surface front is slow to push southeast and in most \nguidance stalls somewhere near eastern Tennessee into NW Georgia\nlate Saturday night into Sunday. However, the better low level \nforcing shifts east into Georgia NNE into the Mid-Atlantic area \ninto early next week. Despite that, an upper low forms in the \nbase of the upper level trough axis over the area late Saturday \nnight into early next week. This will provide some good upper \nlevel lift. Intensity of precipitation should be lighter (due to \nlower moisture content and forcing throughout the entire atmospheric\ncolumn). This activity should be fairly widespread and mainly in \nthe afternoon and early evening hours. Expect expansive cloud \ncover to be fairly routine during this timeframe. This and lower \ntemperatures should make for a continuing cool period over the \narea with highs remaining in the 80 to 85 degree range primarily. \n\nThis upper low is pulled north or northwest towards another \nlongwave trough axis on Wednesday. However, low to medium chances \nof showers and thunderstorms should linger over the area. With\nmore morning sunshine and a strong upper ridge beginning to edge \ninto NE ALabama from the east, highs should rebound back into the \nmid to upper 80s.",
